          heading: "The roadmap and its governance",
          body: "A 12-month plan fails in the diary before it fails anywhere else: the year is long, the operation is loud, and untended priorities dissolve into it by June. The countermeasures are phasing and governance.\n\nPhase the year in quarters with different characters. Q1 is foundations and the quick win: baselines locked, owners briefed, the fast-result priority (the checkout fix, the capture-rate scripting) driven hard for early proof. Q2-Q3 carry the structural builds - the membership launch, the recruitment and succession work, the menu re-engineering timed to the seasonal print. Q4 is consolidation and compounding: embedding what worked, killing what did not, and building next year's diagnosis from a position of data rather than scramble. Within quarters, use 30-60-90-day milestones per priority - dated, observable events ('membership product priced and approved', not 'membership progressing').\n\nGovernance is deliberately light and utterly regular: the weekly scorecard (the manager programme's machinery) tracks the operation; a monthly priority review - one hour, owners reporting against milestones, exactly one question per priority ('are we where we said, and if not, what changes?') - tracks the plan; and a quarterly re-forecast connects both to the financial year.\n\nThe governing principle when reality diverges - and it will - comes from the forecasting module: change actions freely, change targets reluctantly, change the strategic outcome only when the business case itself has changed. A plan whose targets move every month is a diary; a plan whose actions never move is a fantasy.",
